## JWT Expiry Check Bug

The most common cause is a **units mismatch**. JWT `exp` is in **seconds** since epoch, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**.

### The Broken Code

```javascript
// ❌ WRONG - comparing milliseconds to seconds
const isExpired = Date.now() > payload.exp;
// Date.now() = 1703980800000 (milliseconds)
// payload.exp = 1703980800 (seconds)
// 1703980800000 > 1703980800 is ALWAYS true... 
// but if you have the logic inverted:

const isValid = Date.now() < payload.exp;
// 1703980800000 < 1703980800 is ALWAYS false
// So isValid is always false... tokens always "invalid"?

// Or worse - the check is backwards entirely:
if (Date.now() < payload.exp) {  // Always false, so block never runs
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
}
// Expired tokens sail right through
```

### The Fix

```javascript
// ✅ CORRECT - convert Date.now() to seconds
const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);

if (nowInSeconds > payload.exp) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
}
```

### Complete Hardened Middleware

```javascript
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');

function authMiddleware(req, res, next) {
    const authHeader = req.headers.authorization;

    if (!authHeader?.startsWith('Bearer ')) {
        return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Missing or malformed token' });
    }

    const token = authHeader.split(' ')[1];

    try {
        // Option A: Let the library handle expiry (recommended)
        // jsonwebtoken checks exp automatically - just don't disable it
        const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, {
            algorithms: ['HS256'],  // Explicitly whitelist algorithm
            // Do NOT pass { ignoreExpiration: true } unless intentional
        });

        req.user = payload;
        next();

    } catch (err) {
        // jsonwebtoken throws specific error types
        if (err.name === 'TokenExpiredError') {
            return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });
        }
        if (err.name === 'JsonWebTokenError') {
            return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid token' });
        }
        if (err.name === 'NotBeforeError') {
            return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token not yet valid' });
        }
        return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Authentication error' });
    }
}

// Option B: Manual check if you decode without verifying
function manualExpiryCheck(payload) {
    if (!payload.exp) {
        throw new Error('Token has no expiry - reject it');
    }

    const nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
    const clockSkewBuffer = 30; // 30 seconds tolerance for clock drift

    if (nowInSeconds > payload.exp + clockSkewBuffer) {
        throw new Error('Token expired');
    }
}

module.exports = authMiddleware;
```

### Other Things to Check

| Issue | Symptom | Fix |
|-------|---------|-----|
| `ignoreExpiration: true` passed to `jwt.verify()` | All tokens valid | Remove that option |
| Using `jwt.decode()` instead of `jwt.verify()` | No validation at all | Switch to `jwt.verify()` |
| Wrong secret/key | Verification always fails, fallback logic skips checks | Verify `JWT_SECRET` is set correctly |
| `exp` field missing from token | No expiry enforced | Reject tokens without `exp` |
| Logic operator inverted (`<` vs `>`) | Expired = valid, valid = expired | Flip the operator |

### Key Takeaway

**Prefer `jwt.verify()` over manual checks** — it handles expiry, signature validation, and algorithm verification in one call. Manual `Date.now()` comparisons are error-prone and easy to get wrong.
